WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.380
This is a pile of paper slips. In about a minute it's going to write a sentence that nobody has ever written.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:16.420
Every slip is a pair of words from a book: the word in the box, and the word that came next. A pair the book says six times gets six slips.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:28.920
Choose a slip to start from. A story starts after a full stop, so take one with a full stop in the box. This one says "it". Write that word down.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:44.320
Now find every slip with "it" in the box. There are ten. Three say "sits", three "watches", three "goes", and one says "is". Pick one at random: "sits".

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.220
Every "sits" slip says "on", and every "on" slip says "the", so there's only one way to go.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:01:05.660
"The" has nineteen slips. Pick one: "dog". And "dog" has four: two full stops, two "runs". Pick one: full stop.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:17.440
"It sits on the dog." The book never says that; the magpie sits on the fence. Nobody wrote this sentence; the slips did, one pair at a time, like dominoes.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.360
And here's everything this pile could have written instead.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:29.960
Every pick sends you down a different path. So chain ten thousand words, cut them back into slips, and sort them.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:42.560
Every chain different. Every chain sounding like the book. That's what Claude or ChatGPT is doing every time it writes a word, with vastly more than a pile of slips.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:58.570
Spread your cutouts out. Write down a starting word, find a slip with that word in the box, and write down the word beside it. Then find a slip with that new word in the box. The colours help you find the matches fast.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.040
Start chaining.
